&lt;p>&lt;strong>Part 23 of the &lt;em>Learning Quantum Physics&lt;/em> series.&lt;/strong>&lt;/p>
&lt;p>In &lt;a href="https://dasarpai.github.io/dsblog/quantum-computing-careers">Quantum Computing Careers&lt;/a> the stack has a layer that is easy to treat as a slogan: &lt;strong>error correction&lt;/strong>. This article is the concept behind that layer.&lt;/p>
&lt;p>The question is not &amp;ldquo;do quantum computers make mistakes?&amp;rdquo; Every computer does. The useful questions are:&lt;/p>
&lt;blockquote>
&lt;p>What, exactly, is being spoiled? Why does it spoil so easily? And what can we do about it without destroying the computation we were trying to run?&lt;/p>&lt;/blockquote></description></item></channel></rss>